=====Translations=====
{{see translation subpage|Adjective}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|farther,further}}

# To, from or over a great distance in space, time or other extent.
#: {{syn|en|long}}
#: {{ux|en|You have all come '''far''' and you will go '''further'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|He built a time machine and travelled '''far''' into the future.}}
#: {{ux|en|Over time, his views moved '''far''' away from mine.}}
#: {{ux|en|You've gone '''far''' enough. Actually, a bit too '''far'''.}}
# Very much; by a great amount.
#: {{ux|en|He was '''far''' richer than we'd thought.}}
#: {{ux|en|The expense '''far''' exceeds what I expected.}}
#: {{ux|en|I saw a tiny figure '''far''' below me.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=May 5, 2012|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/17878435 Chelsea 2-1 Liverpool]
|passage=The Reds were on the back foot early on when a catalogue of defensive errors led to Ramires giving Chelsea the lead. Jay Spearing conceded possession in midfield and Ramires escaped Jose Enrique '''far''' too easily before scoring at the near post with a shot Reina should have saved.}}

=====Usage notes=====
As with the adjective, the adverb sense is often repeated for intensive meaning. A foul-tasting drink may be {{good||'''far far worse'''}} than what one expected.

